var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Reference","page":"Reference","text":"PracticalBayes also re-exports HMC and HMCDA from AdvancedHMC; MCMCThreads, MCMCSerial and MCMCDistributed from AbstractMCMC; AutoForwardDiff, AutoReverseDiff, AutoMooncake and AutoEnzyme from ADTypes; and I from LinearAlgebra.","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Models","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Models"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Distributions","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Distributions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Sampling","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Sampling"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Gibbs-sampling","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Gibbs sampling"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Predictive-draws-and-densities-at-a-point","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Predictive draws and densities at a point"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Point-estimates","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Point estimates"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Log-densities-and-gradients","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Log densities and gradients"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Samplers-for-trimmed-binaries","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Samplers for trimmed binaries"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/#Evaluation-internals","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Evaluation internals"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.@model","page":"Reference","text":"@model function name(args...; kwargs...) ... end\n\nDefine a model. Calling name(args...) returns a Model.\n\nStatements in the body:\n\nx ~ dist: if x is a model argument holding data, x is observed and logpdf(dist, x) is added to the log-likelihood. Otherwise x is a parameter. Pass missing for an argument to make it a parameter, or bind data later with condition.\nx[i] ~ dist: if x is a model argument, the element x[i] is observed. Otherwise x[i] is one element of an indexed family of parameters; create the container first, for continuous elements with x = Vector{paramtype(__mode__)}(undef, n) (see paramtype).\ny .~ dists: observe every element of the argument y at once, faster than a loop. dists may be one distribution or a broadcast such as Normal.(mu, sigma). .~ cannot introduce parameters; use an array distribution with ~ for those.\nz := expr: assign z, record it in the chain, and treat it as data, so a later z ~ dist observes it.\n@addlogprob!expr: add expr to the log-likelihood.\nreturn expr: the value returned gives back.\n\nDiscrete parameters (Categorical, Bernoulli, ...) cannot be sampled by HMC; give them a Gibbs kernel in Gibbs.\n\n@model function linreg(x, y)\n    α ~ Normal(0, 10)\n    β ~ Normal(0, 10)\n    σ ~ Exponential(1)\n    y .~ Normal.(α .+ β .* x, σ)\nend\n\nm = linreg(x, y)\nchn = sample(m, NUTS(0.8), 1000)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.@model"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.@static_model","page":"Reference","text":"@static_model function name(args...) ... end\n\nDefine a model exactly as @model does, but make build_layout default to static=true: a layout whose site records are held in a Tuple, as juliac --trim needs.\n\nUse it for a model that will be compiled into a standalone binary. Compile time grows with the number of site records, so write an indexed family as one array distribution (x ~ MvNormal(zeros(n), I)) rather than a loop of x[i] ~.\n\nSee the trimming guide.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.@static_model"},{"category":"macro","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.@addlogprob!","page":"Reference","text":"@addlogprob!(expr)\n@addlogprob!(expr, depends=(:a, :b, ...))\n\nAdd expr to the log-likelihood. Valid only inside an @model body.\n\nUse it for a log-density term that is not a ~ statement, such as a likelihood with a discrete latent summed out.\n\ndepends lists the model variables expr reads. A Gibbs HMC block that owns none of them then skips the term, which cannot change its gradient. Name every variable expr reads: a missing name silently drops a gradient term. check_depends tests the annotations. Without depends the term is always evaluated.\n\n@model function m(data)\n    a ~ Normal()\n    b ~ Normal()\n    @addlogprob! loglik_a(a, data) depends=(:a,)\n    @addlogprob! loglik_b(b, data) depends=(:b,)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.@addlogprob!"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.Model","page":"Reference","text":"Model\n\nA model with its arguments bound, as returned by calling a function defined with @model.\n\nFields:\n\nf: the evaluator the macro generated.\nargs: the call's arguments as a NamedTuple. An argument holding missing is a parameter, not data.\nconditioned: data bound with condition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.Model"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.condition","page":"Reference","text":"condition(model, nt::NamedTuple) -> Model\nmodel | nt -> Model\n\nReturn a copy of model in which the variables named in nt are observed with the given values. Only whole variables can be conditioned, not elements.\n\njulia> @model function demo()\n           μ ~ Normal(0, 1)\n           y ~ Normal(μ, 1)\n       end;\n\njulia> m = demo() | (; y = 0.5);\n\njulia> logjoint(m, (; μ = 0.0)) ≈ logpdf(Normal(), 0.0) + logpdf(Normal(), 0.5)\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.condition"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.decondition","page":"Reference","text":"decondition(model, names::Symbol...) -> Model\ndecondition(model) -> Model\n\nReturn a copy of model in which the variables names, previously bound with condition, are parameters again. With no names, all conditioning is removed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.decondition"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.filldist","page":"Reference","text":"filldist(dist::Distribution, dim::Int, dims::Int...)\n\nAn array of dim × dims... independent copies of dist, as one array-valued distribution for a single ~ site. Equivalent to product_distribution(fill(dist, dim, dims...)).\n\njulia> size(rand(filldist(Normal(), 3, 2)))\n(3, 2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.filldist"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.arraydist","page":"Reference","text":"arraydist(dists::AbstractArray{<:Distribution})\n\nAn array of independent, not necessarily identical, distributions as one array-valued distribution. Equivalent to product_distribution(dists).\n\nUse it for a ~ or .~ site whose elements each have their own parameters:\n\ny ~ arraydist(Normal.(X * beta, sigma))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.arraydist"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.Flat","page":"Reference","text":"Flat()\n\nImproper flat prior over the real line: logpdf(Flat(), x) == 0 for every x.\n\njulia> logpdf(Flat(), 3.0)\n0.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.Flat"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.FlatPos","page":"Reference","text":"FlatPos(l)\n\nImproper flat prior over (l, Inf): logpdf is 0 above l and -Inf at or below it.\n\njulia> logpdf(FlatPos(0.0), 2.0), logpdf(FlatPos(0.0), -1.0)\n(0.0, -Inf)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.FlatPos"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.LogPoisson","page":"Reference","text":"LogPoisson(logλ)\n\nPoisson(exp(logλ)), parameterised by the log-rate.\n\njulia> logpdf(LogPoisson(log(3.0)), 2) ≈ logpdf(Poisson(3.0), 2)\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.LogPoisson"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.BinomialLogit","page":"Reference","text":"BinomialLogit(n, logitp)\n\nBinomial(n, logistic(logitp)), parameterised by the logit of the success probability.\n\njulia> logpdf(BinomialLogit(10, 0.0), 5) ≈ logpdf(Binomial(10, 0.5), 5)\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.BinomialLogit"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.BernoulliCLogLog","page":"Reference","text":"BernoulliCLogLog(η)\n\nBernoulli(p) with p = 1 - exp(-exp(η)), the complementary log-log link.\n\nη is a log-hazard, so this is the usual likelihood for discrete-time survival or force-of-infection data. Computing on the η scale stays accurate for large |η|.\n\njulia> logpdf(BernoulliCLogLog(0.0), 1) ≈ log(1 - exp(-1))\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.BernoulliCLogLog"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#StatsBase.sample-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Model, AdvancedHMC.AbstractHMCSampler, Integer}","page":"Reference","text":"sample(rng, model::Model, spl::AdvancedHMC.AbstractHMCSampler, N;\n       adtype=AutoForwardDiff(), init=NamedTuple(), chain_type=SymChain,\n       kwargs...)\n\nDraw N samples from the posterior of model with an HMC sampler such as NUTS() and return them as a SymChain.\n\nKeywords\n\nn_adapts: number of warm-up iterations that adapt the step size and metric; defaults to min(1000, N ÷ 2).\ndiscard_initial: iterations to run and drop before the N kept ones; defaults to n_adapts, so the chain holds N draws after warm-up. Pass discard_initial=0 to keep the warm-up.\nthinning: keep every thinning-th iteration.\ninit: starting values by name; others are drawn from the prior.\nadtype: AD backend for the gradient, e.g. AutoMooncake().\nprogress: show a progress meter; defaults to default_progress.\nchain_type=nothing: return AdvancedHMC's raw transitions instead.\n\nParameters are reported on their constrained scale. Sampler statistics such as :acceptance_rate and :n_steps, and the :logprior, :loglikelihood and :logjoint of each draw, are included as extras. The parameters take the sampler's number type, so NUTS(0.8f0) samples in Float32 (see Run a model in Float32).\n\nFor several chains: sample(model, spl, MCMCThreads(), N, nchains).\n\nchn = sample(model, NUTS(), 1000)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsBase.sample"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#StatsBase.sample-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Model, Gibbs, Integer}","page":"Reference","text":"sample(rng, model::Model, spl::Gibbs, N; n_adapts=0, discard_initial=0,\n       init=NamedTuple(), adtype=AutoForwardDiff(), save_states=nothing,\n       progress=default_progress(), chain_type=SymChain)\n\nRun N sweeps of the Gibbs sampler spl after discard_initial sweeps of burn-in, and return them as a SymChain.\n\nKeywords\n\nn_adapts: warm-up iterations for each HMC block, counted from the first sweep.\ndiscard_initial: sweeps to run and drop before the N kept ones.\ninit: starting values by name; others are drawn from the prior.\nadtype: AD backend used by every HMC block.\nsave_states: where each variable's draws go, by name. :chain (the default) keeps them in the chain; :buffer leaves them out; (path, every) writes them to disk every every sweeps (needs using JLD2; read them back with read_states).\nchain_type=nothing: return a vector of NamedTuples instead.\n\nchn = sample(model, spl, 1000; n_adapts=500, discard_initial=500,\n             save_states=(z = :buffer,))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsBase.sample"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#StatsBase.sample-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Model, AbstractMCMC.AbstractSampler, Integer}","page":"Reference","text":"sample(rng, model::Model, spl::AbstractMCMC.AbstractSampler, N;\n       adtype=AutoForwardDiff(), init=NamedTuple(), chain_type=SymChain,\n       kwargs...)\n\nDraw N samples with any AbstractMCMC sampler that works on a log density and implements AbstractMCMC.getparams, such as AdvancedMH's random-walk sampler or a slice sampler. Returns a SymChain.\n\nThe sampler works on the unconstrained parameter vector, with a gradient from adtype for samplers that use one. discard_initial and thinning have their AbstractMCMC meanings; nothing is discarded by default. Every parameter must be continuous; for discrete ones use Gibbs with a Gibbs kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatsBase.sample"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.NUTS","page":"Reference","text":"NUTS(δ=0.65; adtype=nothing, max_depth=10, Δ_max=1000.0, integrator=:leapfrog, metric=:diagonal)\n\nThe No-U-Turn sampler with target acceptance rate δ. Without adtype this is AdvancedHMC's NUTS, and the other keywords are passed to it.\n\nadtype sets the AD backend, e.g. NUTS(; adtype=AutoMooncake()); inside a Gibbs block it applies to that block alone. A Float32 δ (NUTS(0.8f0)) makes sample run in Float32.\n\nchn = sample(model, NUTS(), 1000)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.NUTS"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#FlexiChains.SymChain","page":"Reference","text":"SymChain\n\nThe chain type sample returns, re-exported from FlexiChains.jl. Index it by parameter name: chn[:μ] is a (draws × chains) matrix. A vector-valued parameter is one entry holding whole vectors; see param_draws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"FlexiChains.SymChain"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.param_draws","page":"Reference","text":"param_draws(chn::FlexiChains.SymChain, name::Symbol) -> Matrix\nparam_draws(chn::FlexiChains.SymChain, name::Symbol, i) -> Matrix\n\nThe draws of one parameter as a plain (draws × chains) Matrix. For a vector-valued parameter, pass the element index i.\n\nA vector parameter is one chain entry whose values are whole vectors, so chn[:beta][:, 2] indexes the second chain, not beta[2]. Use this function instead:\n\nparam_draws(chn, :beta, 2)     # every draw of beta[2], all chains\nparam_draws(chn, :sigma)       # a scalar parameter\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.param_draws"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.chain_draws","page":"Reference","text":"chain_draws(chn::FlexiChains.SymChain) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nEvery draw in chn as a NamedTuple of parameter values, all chains pooled (chain by chain). Sampler statistics are left out. The result is the draws argument predict expects, and each element can be passed to logjoint, returned or pointwise_loglikelihoods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.chain_draws"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.NUTSthenHMC","page":"Reference","text":"NUTSthenHMC(δ=0.8; n_leapfrog=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nAn HMC sampler that runs NUTS for the first n_adapts iterations and then fixed-length HMC with the metric and step size NUTS adapted. Each draw after warm-up then costs the same number of gradient evaluations, which keeps the sweeps of a Gibbs sampler even.\n\nKeywords\n\nn_leapfrog: leapfrog steps per iteration after warm-up. nothing takes 2^d - 1, where d is the median NUTS tree depth during warm-up.\njitter: draw the step count uniformly from 1:n_leapfrog each iteration.\nOthers (metric, max_depth, ...) are passed to AdvancedHMC.NUTS.\n\nn_adapts must be given to sample.\n\nchn = sample(model, NUTSthenHMC(0.8), 2000; n_adapts=1000)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.NUTSthenHMC"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.AdaptiveHMC","page":"Reference","text":"AdaptiveHMC(δ=0.8; n_leapfrog=10, kwargs...)\n\nFixed-length HMC that adapts its step size and metric during the first n_adapts iterations, as NUTS does, targeting acceptance rate δ. Every iteration takes n_leapfrog leapfrog steps.\n\nKeywords\n\nn_leapfrog: leapfrog steps per iteration.\njitter: draw the step count uniformly from 1:n_leapfrog each iteration.\nmetric: :diagonal (default), :dense, :unit, or an AdvancedHMC metric.\nadapt: :full adapts the metric and step size; :step_size keeps the metric and adapts only the step size (see warm_start).\n\nchn = sample(model, AdaptiveHMC(0.8; n_leapfrog=16), 2000; n_adapts=1000)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.AdaptiveHMC"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.JitteredNSteps","page":"Reference","text":"JitteredNSteps(L)\n\nAdvancedHMC termination criterion for a fixed-length trajectory whose step count is drawn uniformly from 1:L at each iteration. A drop-in replacement for FixedNSteps(L) in a Trajectory{EndPointTS}. It avoids a fixed trajectory length resonating with the posterior, and costs (L + 1) / 2 steps per iteration on average.\n\nAdaptiveHMC and NUTSthenHMC use it when jitter=true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.JitteredNSteps"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.default_progress","page":"Reference","text":"default_progress()\n\nWhether sample shows a progress meter when progress is not passed.\n\ntrue in an interactive session and false in scripts and when the CI environment variable is set, unless setprogress! has been called.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.default_progress"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.setprogress!","page":"Reference","text":"setprogress!(progress::Bool)\n\nTurn the progress meter on or off for every later sample call that does not pass progress, for the rest of the session.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.setprogress!"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.Gibbs","page":"Reference","text":"Gibbs(pairs::Pair...)\n\nA Gibbs sampler that updates blocks of variables in turn, once each per sweep.\n\nEach pair maps a variable name, or a tuple of names, to the sampler for that block:\n\nan HMC sampler (NUTS, HMC, AdaptiveHMC, NUTSthenHMC) or another AbstractMCMC sampler that works on a log density, which takes one step on the block's continuous parameters with every other variable held fixed;\na Gibbs kernel (PG, ConjugateGibbs or your own AbstractGibbsKernel), which draws the block's new values directly.\n\nEvery parameter of the model must be in exactly one block, and discrete parameters need a Gibbs kernel. Run it with sample; pass n_adapts there and keep it the same for the whole run.\n\nspl = Gibbs((:α, :β) => NUTS(), :p => pk, :z => AllocationKernel())\nchn = sample(model, spl, 1000; n_adapts=500, discard_initial=500)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.Gibbs"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.AbstractGibbsKernel","page":"Reference","text":"AbstractGibbsKernel\n\nSupertype of Gibbs kernels: samplers that draw a block of a Gibbs sampler directly. A subtype must implement gibbs_step. A kernel can update any block, discrete or continuous.\n\nGibbs calls the kernel once per sweep and never differentiates through it, so a kernel may use any code: discrete draws, exact forward-filtering backward-sampling, Metropolis proposals.\n\nMutable state inside a kernel must survive deepcopy, which MCMCThreads uses to give each chain its own sampler.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.AbstractGibbsKernel"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.gibbs_step","page":"Reference","text":"gibbs_step(rng, kernel::AbstractGibbsKernel, names::Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}},\n            cond::ModelConditional) -> NamedTuple\n\nDraw new values for the block names given the rest of the state in cond (a ModelConditional). Implement this for your AbstractGibbsKernel subtype.\n\nReturn a NamedTuple with exactly the keys in names. Each value must have the same type as the variable's starting value, e.g. Bool for a Bernoulli variable and Int for a Categorical one.\n\nThe call happens once per Gibbs sweep, outside any gradient computation, so the values in cond are plain numbers.\n\nExample\n\nA two-component mixture whose allocations z are drawn exactly given p and μ:\n\n@model function mixture(y)\n    p ~ Beta(1, 1)\n    μ ~ Normal(0, 5)\n    z = Vector{Bool}(undef, length(y))\n    for i in eachindex(y)\n        z[i] ~ Bernoulli(p)\n    end\n    y .~ Normal.(ifelse.(z, μ, 0.0), 1.0)\nend\n\nstruct AllocationKernel <: AbstractGibbsKernel end\n\nfunction PracticalBayes.gibbs_step(rng, ::AllocationKernel, names, cond::ModelConditional)\n    y, p, μ = cond.model.args.y, cond.values.p, cond.values.μ\n    z = map(y) do yi\n        l1 = log(p) + logpdf(Normal(μ, 1), yi)\n        l0 = log1p(-p) + logpdf(Normal(0, 1), yi)\n        rand(rng) < 1 / (1 + exp(l0 - l1))\n    end\n    return (; z = z)\nend\n\nspl = Gibbs(:p => NUTS(0.8), :μ => NUTS(0.8), :z => AllocationKernel())\nchn = sample(mixture(y), spl, 1000; n_adapts=500, discard_initial=500)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.gibbs_step"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.ModelConditional","page":"Reference","text":"ModelConditional(model, values)\n\nThe state a gibbs_step call conditions on.\n\ncond.values: the current value of every parameter, including the block's own.\ncond.model.args: the model's arguments, which hold the data.\ncond.model.conditioned: data bound with condition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.ModelConditional"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.ConjugateGibbs","page":"Reference","text":"ConjugateGibbs(name, family, prior, count; n=nothing, collect=identity)\n\nA Gibbs kernel that draws the block name from a conjugate Beta or Dirichlet full conditional: the prior hyperparameters plus counts returned by count.\n\nArguments\n\nname: the block's variable.\nfamily: :beta or :dirichlet.\nprior: (a, b) for :beta, a concentration vector for :dirichlet.\ncount: count(c) returns the counts given the ModelConditional c: (successes, failures) for :beta, a vector of category counts for :dirichlet.\nn: for n independent draws (one per group, say), count(c, k) is called for each k in 1:n.\ncollect: with n, turns the vector of draws into the block's value.\n\nThe prior in the model should match prior; the kernel does not read it.\n\npk = ConjugateGibbs(:p, :beta, (1, 1)) do c\n    s = count(c.values.z)\n    (s, length(c.values.z) - s)\nend\nspl = Gibbs(:p => pk, :μ => NUTS(0.8), :z => AllocationKernel())\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.ConjugateGibbs"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.PG","page":"Reference","text":"PG(n_particles)\n\nA Gibbs kernel that draws a Gibbs block by particle Gibbs (conditional sequential Monte Carlo) with n_particles particles, at least 2. Use it for discrete latent variables that have no exact kernel.\n\nspl = Gibbs(:k => PG(50), (:μ, :w) => NUTS())\nchn = sample(model, spl, 1000)\n\nMore particles mix faster per sweep. Each sweep re-runs the model once per particle per observation, so the cost grows with the square of the number of observe statements. For hidden Markov models and similar chains an exact gibbs_step kernel is faster and mixes better.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.PG"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.GibbsState","page":"Reference","text":"GibbsState(values, subs)\n\nThe state of a Gibbs sampler between sweeps, as returned by AbstractMCMC.step. values holds the current value of every parameter; subs holds each block's sampler state. Read the HMC tuning from it with hmc_tuning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.GibbsState"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#AbstractMCMC.step-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Model, Gibbs}","page":"Reference","text":"AbstractMCMC.step(rng, model::Model, spl::Gibbs; init=NamedTuple(), adtype=AutoForwardDiff(), kwargs...)\n\nTake the first Gibbs sweep. Checks that every parameter is in exactly one block, starts from init or the prior, and returns (values, state). Every HMC block uses the same adtype.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractMCMC.step"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#AbstractMCMC.step-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Model, Gibbs, GibbsState}","page":"Reference","text":"AbstractMCMC.step(rng, model::Model, spl::Gibbs, state::GibbsState; n_adapts=0, kwargs...)\n\nTake one Gibbs sweep: update each block in the order given to Gibbs. Returns (values, state).\n\nPass the same n_adapts on every call, starting with the first: the adaptation schedule is fixed from the value given then.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractMCMC.step"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.hmc_tuning","page":"Reference","text":"hmc_tuning(spl::Gibbs, state::GibbsState) -> Tuple\n\nThe metric and step size each HMC block of spl has reached in state, one entry per block: nothing for a kernel block, otherwise (names, metric, step_size). metric is the inverse mass matrix as a plain array (a Matrix for a dense metric, a Vector for a diagonal one, nothing for the unit metric).\n\nPlain arrays rather than AdvancedHMC objects, so the tuning serialises and can start a related run through warm_start: the same model refitted to slightly different data needs little or no warm-up once it starts from a metric learnt on the first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.hmc_tuning"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.warm_start","page":"Reference","text":"warm_start(spl::Gibbs, tuning; adapt=:step_size) -> Gibbs\n\nspl with each HMC block starting from the metric and step size in tuning (from hmc_tuning) instead of a unit metric and a step-size search. Pair it with init = state.values to start from where the earlier run ended as well.\n\nWhat warm-up then does is set by n_adapts at the sample call:\n\nn_adapts = 0: the carried metric and step size are used as they are.\nn_adapts > 0 and adapt = :step_size: the step size is re-tuned by dual averaging and the metric is kept. This is the cheap choice when the target has moved a little, since the step size is what a shift in scale upsets.\nn_adapts > 0 and adapt = :full: the whole windowed adaptation runs again. The windowed estimate of the metric restarts from its first window, so this buys little over a cold start beyond skipping the step-size search.\n\n:step_size needs AdaptiveHMC blocks. A NUTS block takes the carried metric and step size under :full only. A plain HMC block adapts nothing, so it is left as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.warm_start"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.check_depends","page":"Reference","text":"check_depends(model, spl::Gibbs; init=NamedTuple(), adtype=AutoForwardDiff(),\n              rng=Random.default_rng(), atol=0.0, verbose=true) -> DependsReport\n\nCheck the depends= annotations of @addlogprob! in model under the blocks of spl. For each HMC block, the gradient with terms skipped is compared with the gradient with nothing skipped; any difference means a depends list is missing a variable.\n\nReturns a DependsReport; report.ok is false on a mismatch. The check runs at one point, the starting values or init, so for a model whose dependencies change with the parameters run it at several. Leave atol at 0.0 unless the AD backend is not deterministic.\n\nreport = check_depends(model, spl)\nreport.ok || error(\"a depends= annotation is incomplete\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.check_depends"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.DependsReport","page":"Reference","text":"DependsReport\n\nThe result of check_depends. ok is true when every block's gradient matched. rows has one entry per HMC block: the largest gradient difference and, if non-zero, the variables likely missing from a depends list.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.DependsReport"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.SaveToChain","page":"Reference","text":"Keep the variable's draws in the chain: :chain in save_states, the default.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.SaveToChain"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.SaveToBuffer","page":"Reference","text":"Leave the variable's draws out of the chain and write them nowhere: :buffer in save_states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.SaveToBuffer"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.SaveToDisk","page":"Reference","text":"SaveToDisk(path, every)\n\nOmit the value from the chain and write its draws to disk every every sweeps, one file per write: \"X.jld2\" becomes \"X_iters_1_to_100.jld2\", \"X_iters_101_to_200.jld2\", and so on. Written as (path, every) in save_states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.SaveToDisk"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.read_states","page":"Reference","text":"read_states(template::AbstractString) -> Vector\n\nRead every draw written by save_states = (name = (template, every),) into one vector, in sweep order. Needs using JLD2.\n\nThis loads the whole run into memory. Each file can also be opened on its own with JLD2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.read_states"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.write_state_chunk!","page":"Reference","text":"write_state_chunk!(disp::SaveToDisk, name::Symbol, chunk::AbstractVector,\n                   first_iter::Integer, last_iter::Integer)\n\nWrite the draws chunk of variable name for sweeps first_iter:last_iter to a new file. Called by sample for a SaveToDisk variable. using JLD2 provides the writer; without a backend it throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.write_state_chunk!"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#Base.rand-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG, Model}","page":"Reference","text":"rand([rng,] model::Model) -> NamedTuple\nrand([rng,] model::Model, n::Integer) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nDraw from the prior predictive distribution. Parameters are drawn from their priors; an observed variable keeps its data if it has any and is drawn from the likelihood otherwise. Returns the parameters, the := values and the drawn observations.\n\nTo simulate a dataset, pass missing for the data argument, or fill(missing, n) for a .~ or looped y[i] ~ statement.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.rand"},{"category":"method","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.Model-Tuple{Random.AbstractRNG}","page":"Reference","text":"(model::Model)([rng])\n\nRun model once with every parameter drawn from its prior and return the model body's return value, as calling a Turing model does (model()). Observe sites with data keep their data. For the drawn values themselves, use rand(model).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.Model"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.predict","page":"Reference","text":"predict([rng,] model::Model, draws) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nPosterior predictive sampling. For each NamedTuple in draws (see chain_draws), set the parameters to that draw and draw every observed variable that has no data from the likelihood.\n\nPass model with the data argument replaced by missing values. For a .~ or looped y[i] ~ statement give the output shape, fill(missing, n); a bare missing works only for y ~. An array with only some elements missing is not supported.\n\nReturns one NamedTuple per draw holding every variable.\n\nchn = sample(linreg(x, y), NUTS(0.8), 1000)\npreds = predict(linreg(x_new, fill(missing, length(x_new))), chain_draws(chn))\n\n\n\n\n\npredict([rng,] model::Model, chn::SymChain; include_all=false) -> SymChain\n\nPosterior predictive sampling from a chain. For every draw in chn, set the parameters to that draw and draw each observed variable that has no data. Returns a chain of the predicted variables, with the same iterations and chains as chn; include_all=true adds the parameters.\n\nFree the observations by passing missing data, predict(linreg(x_new, fill(missing, n)), chn), or by removing conditioning, predict(decondition(model), chn).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.predict"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.returned","page":"Reference","text":"returned(model::Model, nt::NamedTuple; rng=Random.default_rng())\n\nRun model with its parameters set to nt and return the value of the model body's return statement (nothing if it has none). Use it to compute derived quantities at each posterior draw.\n\njulia> @model function demo()\n           m̃ ~ Normal(0, 1)\n           return m̃ + 1\n       end;\n\njulia> returned(demo(), (; m̃ = 2.0))\n3.0\n\n\n\n\n\nreturned(model::Model, chn::SymChain) -> Matrix\n\nThe model body's return value at every draw of chn, as an (iterations × chains) matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.returned"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.logjoint","page":"Reference","text":"logjoint(a::Accum)\n\nlogprior(a) + loglikelihood_(a).\n\n\n\n\n\nlogjoint(cond::ModelConditional; overrides...) -> Real\n\nThe model's log joint density at the current values, with any parameters given as keywords replaced. A Metropolis kernel can use it to score a proposal:\n\nlp_new = logjoint(cond; z = z_proposed)\n\nIt runs the whole model, so an exact update is usually faster.\n\n\n\n\n\nlogjoint(model::Model, nt::NamedTuple) -> Real\n\nThe log joint density of model with its parameters set to nt, for example one posterior draw. Every observed variable must have data.\n\njulia> @model function demo(y)\n           μ ~ Normal(0, 1)\n           y ~ Normal(μ, 1)\n       end;\n\njulia> logjoint(demo(0.5), (; μ = 0.0)) ≈ logpdf(Normal(), 0.0) + logpdf(Normal(), 0.5)\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.logjoint"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.logprior","page":"Reference","text":"logprior(a::Accum)\n\nThe log-prior accumulated in a.\n\n\n\n\n\nlogprior(model::Model, nt::NamedTuple) -> Real\n\nThe log prior density of model with its parameters set to nt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.logprior"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.loglikelihood_at","page":"Reference","text":"loglikelihood_at(model::Model, nt::NamedTuple) -> Real\n\nThe log-likelihood of model's data with its parameters set to nt. Every observed variable must have data. For one value per observation, use pointwise_loglikelihoods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.loglikelihood_at"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.pointwise_loglikelihoods","page":"Reference","text":"pointwise_loglikelihoods(model::Model, nt::NamedTuple; flatten=false)\n    -> NamedTuple or Vector{Float64}\n\nThe log-likelihood of each observation with the parameters set to nt, as needed for PSIS-LOO or WAIC. Every observed variable must have data.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple with one vector per observed variable, or, with flatten=true, one vector of all values in the order the model visits them.\n\njulia> @model function demo(y)\n           μ ~ Normal(0, 1)\n           y .~ Normal(μ, 1)\n       end;\n\njulia> pointwise_loglikelihoods(demo([0.0, 1.0]), (; μ = 0.0)).y ≈ logpdf.(Normal(), [0.0, 1.0])\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.pointwise_loglikelihoods"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.maximum_a_posteriori","page":"Reference","text":"maximum_a_posteriori(model, optimizer; init=NamedTuple(), store=NamedTuple(),\n                     adtype=AutoForwardDiff(), kwargs...) -> PointEstimate\n\nFind the posterior mode of model, optimising over the unconstrained parameters. Needs using Optimization and a solver package; optimizer is a solver such as OptimizationOptimJL.LBFGS().\n\nKeywords\n\ninit: starting values by name.\nstore: values, by name, of parameters to hold fixed.\nadtype: AD backend for the gradient.\nOther keywords go to Optimization.solve.\n\nusing Optimization, OptimizationOptimJL\nest = maximum_a_posteriori(model, LBFGS())\nest.constrained\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.maximum_a_posteriori"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.maximum_likelihood","page":"Reference","text":"maximum_likelihood(model, optimizer; kwargs...) -> PointEstimate\n\nLike maximum_a_posteriori, but maximise the log-likelihood alone, ignoring the priors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.maximum_likelihood"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.laplace_approximation","page":"Reference","text":"laplace_approximation(model, optimizer; kwargs...) -> LaplaceApproximation\n\nFind the posterior mode with maximum_a_posteriori (same arguments) and fit a normal approximation there, with covariance minus the inverse Hessian of the log joint. The approximation is on the unconstrained scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.laplace_approximation"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.PointEstimate","page":"Reference","text":"PointEstimate\n\nThe result of maximum_a_posteriori or maximum_likelihood.\n\nFields: constrained (the estimate by name, on the parameters' own scale), theta (the same point as the unconstrained vector), value (the log joint or log-likelihood there), retcode (the optimiser's return code), and the layout and store it was computed with.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.PointEstimate"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.LaplaceApproximation","page":"Reference","text":"LaplaceApproximation\n\nA normal approximation to the posterior on the unconstrained scale, from laplace_approximation. Fields: map (the PointEstimate it is centred on), hessian of the log joint there, and covariance, which is -inv(hessian).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.LaplaceApproximation"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.laplace_mvnormal","page":"Reference","text":"laplace_mvnormal(la::LaplaceApproximation) -> MvNormal\n\nla as an MvNormal over the unconstrained vector θ. Throws if the covariance is not positive definite, which usually means the optimiser did not reach a maximum. Map draws back to named values with invlink(la.map.layout, θ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.laplace_mvnormal"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.build_layout","page":"Reference","text":"build_layout(model; flat=nothing, values=(), untracked=(), init=NamedTuple(),\n             rng=Random.default_rng(), T=Float64, static=false) -> (layout, θ0, store0)\n\nRun model once to find its parameters and decide where each one lives: in the flat unconstrained vector θ or in a store of constant values.\n\nKeywords\n\nflat: names to put in θ. nothing means every continuous parameter not in values.\nvalues: names to put in the store. Discrete parameters must go here.\nuntracked: names in θ to leave out of invlink output.\ninit: starting values by name; other parameters are drawn from the prior.\nT: element type of θ0, e.g. Float32.\nstatic: hold the site records in a Tuple, for juliac --trim. Defaults to true for an @static_model.\n\nReturns the Layout, the starting vector θ0 and the starting store.\n\njulia> @model function demo(y)\n           μ ~ Normal(0, 1)\n           σ ~ Exponential(1)\n           y ~ Normal(μ, σ)\n       end;\n\njulia> layout, θ0, store0 = build_layout(demo(1.0); T=Float32);\n\njulia> layout.dim, eltype(θ0)\n(2, Float32)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.build_layout"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.Layout","page":"Reference","text":"Layout\n\nWhere each of a model's parameters lives, as built by build_layout.\n\nFields:\n\nslots: for each variable, either a range in the flat unconstrained vector θ or a place in the constant store.\ndim: the length of θ.\nmeta: one record per ~ statement visited, in order.\nuntracked: names sampled as usual but left out of invlink output.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.Layout"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.link","page":"Reference","text":"link(layout, nt::NamedTuple) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nMap constrained parameter values, keyed by name, to the flat unconstrained vector θ described by layout. The inverse of invlink.\n\njulia> @model function demo()\n           σ ~ Exponential(1)\n       end;\n\njulia> layout, _, _ = build_layout(demo());\n\njulia> link(layout, (; σ = 1.0))\n1-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.link"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.invlink","page":"Reference","text":"invlink(layout, θ; include_untracked=false) -> NamedTuple\n\nMap the flat unconstrained vector θ back to constrained values, as a NamedTuple keyed by name. The inverse of link.\n\nNames marked untracked in build_layout are left out unless include_untracked=true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.invlink"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.LogDensityFunction","page":"Reference","text":"LogDensityFunction(model, layout, store=NamedTuple(), adtype=nothing;\n                   θ0=zeros(layout.dim), reject_errors=false, closed_form=true)\n\nThe log joint density of model as a function of the flat unconstrained vector θ, implementing the LogDensityProblems.jl interface. Values in store are held constant.\n\nArguments\n\nlayout, store: from build_layout.\nadtype: an ADTypes backend such as AutoForwardDiff() or AutoMooncake(), AutoPBForwardDiff, GradMode, or nothing for the density only.\n\nKeywords\n\nθ0: a vector of the type you will evaluate at; the gradient is prepared for its element type. Pass a Float32 vector for a Float32 run.\nreject_errors: treat an error inside the model as density -Inf instead of throwing.\nclosed_form: use the closed-form gradient when the model is recognised (see is_gradmode_compatible). Set false to force adtype. Ignored for AutoPBForwardDiff, which always uses Piste.\n\nComputation follows the element type of θ.\n\njulia> using LogDensityProblems, ADTypes\n\njulia> @model function demo()\n           μ ~ Normal(0, 1)\n       end;\n\njulia> layout, θ0, store0 = build_layout(demo());\n\njulia> f = LogDensityFunction(demo(), layout, store0, AutoForwardDiff(); θ0=θ0);\n\njulia> LogDensityProblems.logdensity_and_gradient(f, [1.0])\n(-1.4189385332046727, [-1.0])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.LogDensityFunction"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.paramtype","page":"Reference","text":"paramtype(mode) -> Type{<:Real}\n\nThe number type parameters currently have: the element type of the parameter vector under EvalMode (Float64, Float32, or an AD number type during a gradient), and Float64 in every other mode.\n\nUse it inside a model body, as paramtype(__mode__), wherever the model creates an array that will hold parameter values or that sets the precision of a prior:\n\nx = Vector{paramtype(__mode__)}(undef, n)      # container for x[i] ~ ...\nbeta ~ MvNormal(zeros(paramtype(__mode__), k), I)\n\nA Vector{Float64} container fails under AD, and a zeros(k) mean turns a Float32 run into Float64.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.paramtype"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.AutoPBForwardDiff","page":"Reference","text":"AutoPBForwardDiff(; chunk=8)\n\nA forward-mode AD backend built on Piste.jl, written to compile under juliac --trim. Needs import Piste and Julia 1.12.\n\nimport Piste\nldf = LogDensityFunction(model, layout, store, AutoPBForwardDiff(); θ0=θ0)\n\nA gradient over K parameters costs ceil(K / chunk) model evaluations, so for models with many parameters a reverse-mode backend is faster. Float32 parameters stay Float32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.AutoPBForwardDiff"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.GradMode","page":"Reference","text":"GradMode()\n\nBackend that computes the gradient in closed form, for models recognised as generalised linear models. LogDensityFunction already uses it for a recognised model whatever backend you ask for; pass GradMode() to make that a requirement, so that construction throws if the model is not recognised.\n\nA model is recognised when its linear predictor is visibly linear in the parameters: a sum of X * beta (data matrix times parameter vector), x .* b (data vector times scalar parameter), a[idx] (a parameter indexed by data), a scalar parameter, or data. A product of two parameters is not recognised. It cannot be used for a Gibbs block that holds only part of the model.\n\n@model function poisson_reg(X, y)\n    beta ~ MvNormal(zeros(size(X, 2)), I)\n    y ~ arraydist(LogPoisson.(X * beta))\nend\n\nSee also is_gradmode_compatible, check_gradmode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.GradMode"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.is_gradmode_compatible","page":"Reference","text":"is_gradmode_compatible(model::Model) -> Bool\n\nWhether model was recognised as a generalised linear model with a closed-form gradient (see GradMode). false means the model will use ordinary AD, not that it is not a GLM.\n\njulia> @model function reg(x, y)\n           b ~ Normal(0, 1)\n           y ~ MvNormal(x .* b, 1.0)\n       end;\n\njulia> is_gradmode_compatible(reg([1.0, 2.0], [0.5, 1.5]))\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.is_gradmode_compatible"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.check_gradmode","page":"Reference","text":"check_gradmode(plan, layout, args, ldf; n=8, rtol=1e-6, atol=1e-8, rng, verbose=false) -> Bool\n\nCompare the closed-form gradient with the AD gradient of ldf at n random points and return true if they agree. plan is PracticalBayes.gradmode_plan(model.f), args is model.args, and ldf is built with closed_form=false.\n\nldf = LogDensityFunction(m, layout, store, AutoForwardDiff(); θ0=θ0, closed_form=false)\ncheck_gradmode(PracticalBayes.gradmode_plan(m.f), layout, m.args, ldf)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.check_gradmode"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.static_nuts","page":"Reference","text":"static_nuts(gradf!, x0, n_draws, n_warmup; delta=0.8, seed=1)\n\nNUTS with a diagonal metric, adapting the step size towards acceptance rate delta and the metric during n_warmup iterations. Written without dependencies so that it compiles under juliac --trim.\n\ngradf!(g, x) must write the gradient of the log density at x into g and return the log density. Warm-up draws are discarded. Returns a StaticHMCResult.\n\nwarning: Warning\nIts draws are currently too widely spread on low-dimensional targets. Use static_hmc until this is fixed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.static_nuts"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.static_hmc","page":"Reference","text":"static_hmc(gradf!, x0, inv_mass, eps, L, n_draws, n_warmup; seed=1, jitter=true)\n\nHMC with a fixed diagonal inverse metric inv_mass, step size eps and up to L leapfrog steps, written without dependencies so that it compiles under juliac --trim. Nothing is adapted, so choose eps, L and inv_mass beforehand, for example by running it under the JIT and checking n_divergent and accept_rate.\n\ngradf! is as for static_nuts. The first n_warmup draws are discarded. With jitter=true the step count is drawn from 1:L each iteration; jitter=false always takes L. Returns a StaticHMCResult.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.static_hmc"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.static_gradient","page":"Reference","text":"static_gradient(ldf) -> gradf!\n\nWrap a LogDensityFunction with a gradient as the gradf!(g, x) function the static samplers take. Useful for running them from Julia; a trimmed binary should supply its own gradient instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.static_gradient"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.StaticHMCResult","page":"Reference","text":"StaticHMCResult\n\nDraws and diagnostics from static_nuts or static_hmc.\n\nFields: draws (n_params × n_draws, unconstrained), logp, step_size, inv_mass, n_divergent and accept_rate after warm-up, n_divergent_warmup and accept_rate_warmup during it, and mean_depth (NUTS tree depth; 0.0 for static_hmc).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.StaticHMCResult"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.evaluate","page":"Reference","text":"evaluate(model, mode::AbstractEvalMode, acc::Accum) -> (retval, acc)\n\nRun the model body once under mode, starting from the accumulator acc. Returns the body's return value and the final Accum.\n\nThe higher-level functions (sample, logjoint, predict, ...) call this for you. See Evaluation modes for what each mode does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.evaluate"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.Accum","page":"Reference","text":"Accum{T<:Real}\n\nThe log density accumulated while a model runs, kept as two fields: logprior (including the log-Jacobian of any transform) and loglik.\n\nAccum(0.0) starts both at zero. Read the totals with logprior, loglikelihood_ and logjoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.Accum"},{"category":"function","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.loglikelihood_","page":"Reference","text":"loglikelihood_(a::Accum)\n\nThe log-likelihood accumulated in a. The trailing underscore avoids a clash with Distributions.loglikelihood. For a model at a point, use loglikelihood_at.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.loglikelihood_"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.AbstractEvalMode","page":"Reference","text":"AbstractEvalMode\n\nSupertype of the evaluation modes. A mode decides, at each ~ statement, where a parameter's value comes from and what is recorded. Inside a model body the current mode is __mode__.\n\nSubtypes: TraceMode, EvalMode, PriorMode, FixedMode, PointwiseMode. See Evaluation modes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.AbstractEvalMode"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.TraceMode","page":"Reference","text":"TraceMode(rng, conditioned, init)\n\nMode that runs a model once to find its variables, drawing each parameter from init if given and otherwise from its prior. build_layout uses it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.TraceMode"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.EvalMode","page":"Reference","text":"EvalMode(layout, θ, store, conditioned)\n\nMode used for log-density and gradient evaluation. Parameters in the flat vector are read from θ (unconstrained, with the log-Jacobian added) as laid out by layout; the rest are read from store and held constant. LogDensityFunction uses it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.EvalMode"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.PriorMode","page":"Reference","text":"PriorMode(rng, conditioned)\n\nMode that draws every parameter from its prior and records every variable's value. rand(model) uses it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.PriorMode"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.FixedMode","page":"Reference","text":"FixedMode(rng, fixed, conditioned; predict=false)\n\nMode that reads every parameter from the NamedTuple fixed, such as one posterior draw. With predict=true, observed variables that have no data are drawn from the likelihood; otherwise every observed variable needs data. logjoint, returned and predict use it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.FixedMode"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.PointwiseMode","page":"Reference","text":"PointwiseMode(fixed, conditioned)\n\nMode that reads every parameter from fixed, like FixedMode, and records each observation's log-likelihood separately. pointwise_loglikelihoods uses it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.PointwiseMode"},{"category":"type","location":"reference/#PracticalBayes.ParticleMode","page":"Reference","text":"ParticleMode\n\nEvaluation mode used by PG: replays (or extends) one particle's block values and accumulates every other site's log density, stopping after a set number of weighting sites. names is the tuple of block variable names, a type parameter so membership folds to a constant at each site.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PracticalBayes.ParticleMode"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/map_laplace/#Find-the-MAP-estimate-or-a-Laplace-approximation","page":"Find the MAP estimate or a Laplace approximation","text":"Point estimates need Optimization.jl and a solver package:\n\nusing PracticalBayes, Optimization, OptimizationOptimJL\n\nest = maximum_a_posteriori(model, LBFGS())\nest.constrained          # estimates by name\nest.value                # log joint at the estimate\n\nmaximum_likelihood takes the same arguments and ignores the priors.\n\nTo hold some parameters fixed, pass their values as store:\n\nest = maximum_a_posteriori(model, LBFGS(); store=(; σ = 0.3))\n\nlaplace_approximation adds a normal approximation around the mode. It is on the unconstrained scale, so map draws back with invlink:\n\nla = laplace_approximation(model, LBFGS())\nd = laplace_mvnormal(la)\ndraws = [invlink(la.map.layout, θ) for θ in eachcol(rand(d, 1000))]","title":"Find the MAP estimate or a Laplace approximation"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/#Explanation","page":"Explanation","text":"","title":"Explanation"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/#Gibbs-kernels-in-a-Gibbs-sampler","page":"Explanation","text":"HMC moves continuous parameters using the gradient of the log density, and each iteration evaluates that gradient many times. A discrete or very large latent variable, such as a hidden state trajectory, has no gradient, and differentiating through it would be expensive even if it did.\n\nA Gibbs sampler therefore splits the work. Each sweep updates the blocks in turn. An HMC block sees every other block as fixed data: those values sit in a store passed to the AD backend as constants, so they are never differentiated, and an HMC step never calls a Gibbs kernel. A Gibbs kernel runs once per sweep with plain numbers, so it can use any algorithm: exact conditional draws, forward filtering and backward sampling, or Metropolis proposals scored with logjoint.\n\nThe cost is the usual one for Gibbs sampling: blocks that are strongly correlated mix slowly when updated separately.","title":"Gibbs kernels in a Gibbs sampler"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/#Data-and-parameters:-:","page":"Explanation","text":"Whether x ~ dist observes data or declares a parameter is decided when @model expands the body, from names alone: x is data if it is one of the model's arguments, and a parameter otherwise. Deciding this once, rather than checking at every evaluation, is part of what keeps an observe statement as cheap as a logpdf call.\n\nThe rule needs a way to say that a name computed inside the body is data too. That is what := does. z := expr assigns the value, adds z to the names treated as data for the rest of the body, so a later z ~ dist (or z[i] ~ dist) observes the computed value, and records z in the chain:\n\n@model function f(x)\n    μ ~ Normal(0, 1)\n    z := x .- 1      # data, computed from an argument\n    z .~ Normal(μ, 1)\nend\n\nWith = instead, z ~ dist would declare z a parameter, and z would not appear in the chain. := is also the way to keep a derived quantity, such as a transformed parameter, with each draw. rand and predict return := values too, recomputed from each draw, so yhat := X * beta follows new data passed to predict.","title":"Data and parameters: :="},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/#Skipping-terms-in-Gibbs-blocks:-depends","page":"Explanation","text":"An HMC block of a Gibbs sampler evaluates the model's whole log density and its gradient with respect to the block's own parameters. A term that does not involve any of those parameters is a constant for that block: it shifts the log density by a fixed amount and adds nothing to the gradient, so evaluating it is wasted work. For an expensive @addlogprob! term, such as the likelihood of a latent trajectory, that waste can dominate a block that owns only a few cheap parameters.\n\n@addlogprob! expr depends=(:a, :b) says which variables expr reads. Within a block, the check is resolved when the code is compiled: if the block owns none of a and b, the term is removed from that block's evaluation entirely. Skipping it is exact, since the term could not change that block's update.\n\nThe annotation is not checked automatically. If it leaves out a variable the term reads, the blocks owning that variable lose a real part of their gradient and sample the wrong distribution, without any error. check_depends compares each block's gradient with and without skipping and reports any difference. A whole-model sampler such as plain NUTS owns every variable, so it never skips anything.","title":"Skipping terms in Gibbs blocks: depends="},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/#Evaluation-modes","page":"Explanation","text":"@model turns the model body into a function that takes an evaluation mode as its first argument. Every ~ statement asks the mode what to do: where the parameter's value comes from and what to add to the log density. The mode is a type, so for a given model and mode Julia compiles the choice away and an observe statement costs what logpdf costs.\n\nMode Used by Parameters come from\nTraceMode build_layout init or the prior\nEvalMode LogDensityFunction, sampling the vector θ or the store\nPriorMode rand(model) the prior\nFixedMode logjoint, returned, predict a NamedTuple\nPointwiseMode pointwise_loglikelihoods a NamedTuple\n\nPriorMode, and FixedMode when called from predict, also draw observed variables that have no data.\n\nbuild_layout runs the model once in TraceMode to find its parameters and fix where each lives: a range of the unconstrained vector θ, or the store of constants. Sampling then only ever runs EvalMode with that fixed layout.","title":"Evaluation modes"},{"category":"section","location":"explanation/#Gradient-paths","page":"Explanation","text":"LogDensityFunction gets its gradient in one of three ways.\n\nAD through DifferentiationInterface. Any backend DifferentiationInterface supports, chosen with adtype: ForwardDiff by default, or Mooncake, Enzyme and others. The gradient is prepared once, for the element type of θ0.\nThe closed form. When @model sees that a model is a generalised linear model, with a linear predictor that is visibly linear in the parameters, it records the model's structure. The gradient is then computed directly, essentially X' * residual, without AD. This happens automatically, except with AutoPBForwardDiff, which is kept for trimming; the recognition is purely syntactic and conservative, so a model it does not recognise uses AD. See GradMode and check_gradmode.\nPiste forward mode. AutoPBForwardDiff computes derivatives with dual numbers whose sizes are fixed at compile time and needs nothing built at run time, which is what juliac --trim requires.\n\nThe static samplers, static_nuts and static_hmc, exist for the same reason: AdvancedHMC does not compile under --trim, so these are written with no dependencies and take a gradient function you supply. See Compile a model into a standalone binary.","title":"Gradient paths"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"Here we fit a straight line to simulated data: we write a model, sample its posterior with NUTS, look at the result, and check the fit with posterior predictive draws.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Simulate-some-data","page":"Getting started","text":"using PracticalBayes, Distributions, StableRNGs, Statistics\n\nrng = StableRNG(1)\nx = collect(range(0, 1; length=50))\ny = 1.0 .+ 2.0 .* x .+ 0.3 .* randn(rng, 50)\n\nThe true intercept is 1, the slope 2 and the noise standard deviation 0.3.","title":"Simulate some data"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Write-the-model","page":"Getting started","text":"A model is a function marked with @model. Each ~ line gives a variable a distribution. α, β and σ are not arguments, so they are parameters. y is an argument holding data, so .~ observes each element of it.\n\n@model function linreg(x, y)\n    α ~ Normal(0, 10)\n    β ~ Normal(0, 10)\n    σ ~ Exponential(1)\n    y .~ Normal.(α .+ β .* x, σ)\nend\n\nmodel = linreg(x, y)","title":"Write the model"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Sample","page":"Getting started","text":"We draw 1000 samples with NUTS. Before them, sample runs warm-up iterations that tune the sampler and are then dropped.\n\nchn = sample(StableRNG(2), model, NUTS(), 1000; progress=false)\n[ Info: Found initial step size 0.0125","title":"Sample"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Look-at-the-result","page":"Getting started","text":"The chain is indexed by parameter name. Each entry is a matrix with one row per draw and one column per chain.\n\nmean(chn[:α]), mean(chn[:β]), mean(chn[:σ])\n(0.92640164385104, 2.1449763129695456, 0.28243277693857133)\n\nThe posterior means are close to the values we simulated from.","title":"Look at the result"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Check-the-fit","page":"Getting started","text":"To draw new data from the fitted model we build it again with the data replaced by missing values. fill(missing, 50) gives the shape of the output. predict then draws y once for each posterior draw, and returns them as a chain.\n\npreds = predict(StableRNG(3), linreg(x, fill(missing, 50)), chn)\nyrep = reduce(hcat, preds[:y])\nsize(yrep)\n(50, 1000)\n\nEach column of yrep is one simulated dataset. If the model fits, about 90% of the observations should fall inside their 90% predictive intervals:\n\nlo = [quantile(r, 0.05) for r in eachrow(yrep)]\nhi = [quantile(r, 0.95) for r in eachrow(yrep)]\nmean(lo .<= y .<= hi)\n0.92","title":"Check the fit"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Next-steps","page":"Getting started","text":"Mix NUTS with your own samplers: Sample with Gibbs blocks.\nMore predictive checks and LOO: Check a model with predictive draws.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gibbs/#Sample-with-Gibbs-blocks","page":"Sample with Gibbs blocks","text":"A Gibbs sampler splits a model's parameters into blocks and updates each block in turn, holding the others fixed. A block is updated by an HMC sampler or by a Gibbs kernel, which draws the block's new values directly: a built-in one such as ConjugateGibbs or PG, or one you write. Discrete parameters need a Gibbs kernel.\n\nThe example is a two-component mixture: each observation comes from Normal(μ, 1) with probability p or from Normal(0, 1) otherwise, and the allocations z are discrete.\n\nusing PracticalBayes, Distributions, StableRNGs, Statistics\n\ny = vcat(randn(StableRNG(1), 60), 3 .+ randn(StableRNG(2), 40))\n\n@model function mixture(y)\n    p ~ Beta(1, 1)\n    μ ~ Normal(0, 5)\n    z = Vector{Bool}(undef, length(y))\n    for i in eachindex(y)\n        z[i] ~ Bernoulli(p)\n    end\n    y .~ Normal.(ifelse.(z, μ, 0.0), 1.0)\nend","title":"Sample with Gibbs blocks"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gibbs/#Write-a-Gibbs-kernel","page":"Sample with Gibbs blocks","text":"Subtype AbstractGibbsKernel and add a method to gibbs_step. cond.values holds the current value of every parameter, and cond.model.args the model's arguments. Return a NamedTuple with the block's new values.\n\nstruct AllocationKernel <: AbstractGibbsKernel end\n\nfunction PracticalBayes.gibbs_step(rng, ::AllocationKernel, names, cond::ModelConditional)\n    y, p, μ = cond.model.args.y, cond.values.p, cond.values.μ\n    z = map(y) do yi\n        l1 = log(p) + logpdf(Normal(μ, 1), yi)\n        l0 = log1p(-p) + logpdf(Normal(0, 1), yi)\n        rand(rng) < 1 / (1 + exp(l0 - l1))\n    end\n    return (; z = z)\nend\n\nReturn each value with the type it started with: here Bool, because rand(Bernoulli(p)) gives a Bool.","title":"Write a Gibbs kernel"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gibbs/#Use-a-conjugate-block","page":"Sample with Gibbs blocks","text":"p has a Beta full conditional given z, so it can be drawn exactly with ConjugateGibbs. The function passed to it returns the counts of successes and failures.\n\npk = ConjugateGibbs(:p, :beta, (1, 1)) do c\n    s = count(c.values.z)\n    (s, length(c.values.z) - s)\nend","title":"Use a conjugate block"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gibbs/#Use-particle-Gibbs-or-another-sampler","page":"Sample with Gibbs blocks","text":"For a discrete block with no exact kernel, PG draws it by particle Gibbs: :z => PG(50). A continuous block can also use any AbstractMCMC sampler that works on a log density, such as AdvancedMH's random-walk sampler.","title":"Use particle Gibbs or another sampler"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gibbs/#Run-the-sampler","page":"Sample with Gibbs blocks","text":"Give every parameter to exactly one block. To put several continuous parameters in one HMC block, use a tuple of names: (:a, :b) => NUTS(0.8).\n\nspl = Gibbs(:μ => NUTS(0.8), :p => pk, :z => AllocationKernel())\nchn = sample(StableRNG(3), mixture(y), spl, 1000;\n             n_adapts=500, discard_initial=500, progress=false)\nmean(chn[:μ]), mean(chn[:p])\n(3.01446405967907, 0.36655923528610085)\n\nThe data were simulated with μ = 3 and p = 0.4. Unlike a plain NUTS run, a Gibbs run does no warm-up unless you ask: pass n_adapts and discard_initial. n_adapts counts sweeps from the first one, so pass the same value for the whole run.","title":"Run the sampler"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gibbs/#Keep-large-latent-variables-out-of-the-chain","page":"Sample with Gibbs blocks","text":"By default every sweep's z is stored in the chain. For a large latent variable, use save_states to leave it out or write it to disk:\n\nsample(model, spl, 1000; save_states=(z = :buffer,))         # not stored\nusing JLD2\nsample(model, spl, 1000; save_states=(z = (\"z.jld2\", 100),)) # to disk every 100 sweeps\nzs = read_states(\"z.jld2\")","title":"Keep large latent variables out of the chain"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gibbs/#Skip-terms-a-block-does-not-need","page":"Sample with Gibbs blocks","text":"If an @addlogprob! term reads only some parameters, list them with depends. An HMC block owning none of them then skips the term. Check the lists with check_depends:\n\n@addlogprob! expensive_loglik(a, data) depends=(:a,)\n\ncheck_depends(model, spl).ok","title":"Skip terms a block does not need"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gibbs/#Start-from-an-earlier-run's-tuning","page":"Sample with Gibbs blocks","text":"hmc_tuning reads the step size and metric each HMC block has reached from a GibbsState, and warm_start builds a sampler that starts from them. The state comes from stepping the sampler yourself:\n\nimport AbstractMCMC, Random\nrng = Random.default_rng()\nspl = Gibbs(:μ => AdaptiveHMC(0.8; n_leapfrog=8), :p => pk, :z => AllocationKernel())\n\nt, state = AbstractMCMC.step(rng, mixture(y), spl; n_adapts=500)\nfor _ in 2:1000\n    t, state = AbstractMCMC.step(rng, mixture(y), spl, state; n_adapts=500)\nend\n\nspl2 = warm_start(spl, hmc_tuning(spl, state))\nchn = sample(rng, mixture(y_new), spl2, 1000; init=state.values, n_adapts=100)\n\nWith AdaptiveHMC blocks, n_adapts > 0 re-tunes only the step size. init=state.values needs y_new to have the same length as y, since z has one entry per observation.","title":"Start from an earlier run's tuning"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gibbs/#A-larger-example","page":"Sample with Gibbs blocks","text":"EpidemicTrajectories.jl uses Gibbs kernels to sample the hidden infection states of an epidemic model with forward filtering and backward sampling.","title":"A larger example"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gpu/#Keep-data-on-a-GPU","page":"Keep data on a GPU","text":"warning: Untested\nThis path has not been run on GPU hardware. It is a work in progress; expect problems and check results against a CPU run.\n\nThe parameters stay on the CPU and the data lives on a CUDA device, so the model's matrix products and observe statements run on the GPU. Build the data as CuArrays before creating the model:\n\nusing PracticalBayes, Distributions, LinearAlgebra\nimport CUDA\nCUDA.allowscalar(false)\n\n@model function gpu_reg(X, y)\n    k = size(X, 2)\n    beta ~ MvNormal(zeros(paramtype(__mode__), k), 1f0 * I)\n    sigma ~ Exponential(1f0)\n    y ~ MvNormal(X * beta, sigma^2 * I)\nend\n\nmodel = gpu_reg(CUDA.CuArray(Float32.(X)), CUDA.CuArray(Float32.(y)))\n\nWrite the likelihood as whole-array operations, as above: one matrix product and one array-valued observe. A loop over elements indexes the GPU array one scalar at a time; CUDA.allowscalar(false) turns that into an error so you find it early. PracticalBayes itself does not index your data element by element.\n\nSample as on the CPU; see Run a model in Float32 for Float32 parameters.","title":"Keep data on a GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/gpu/#Check-that-the-GPU-was-used","page":"Keep data on a GPU","text":"The repository's GPU tests skip themselves when no GPU is available, so a passing run proves nothing on its own. Run test/gpu/runtests_gpu.jl on a GPU node and check that its last line reads GPU verification: REAL. If CUDA.functional() is false, compare the node's driver version (nvidia-smi) with what CUDA.jl requires.","title":"Check that the GPU was used"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/predictive/#Check-a-model-with-predictive-draws","page":"Check a model with predictive draws","text":"The examples use this model and a chain chn sampled from it:\n\n@model function linreg(x, y)\n    α ~ Normal(0, 10)\n    β ~ Normal(0, 10)\n    σ ~ Exponential(1)\n    y .~ Normal.(α .+ β .* x, σ)\nend","title":"Check a model with predictive draws"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/predictive/#Draw-from-the-prior-predictive","page":"Check a model with predictive draws","text":"Pass missing data and call rand. Each draw holds the parameters and a simulated y.\n\nsims = rand(linreg(x, fill(missing, length(x))), 100)\nsims[1].y","title":"Draw from the prior predictive"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/predictive/#Draw-from-the-posterior-predictive","page":"Check a model with predictive draws","text":"Call predict on the model with missing data. It returns a chain of the predicted variables:\n\npreds = predict(linreg(x_new, fill(missing, length(x_new))), chn)\nyrep = reduce(hcat, preds[:y])    # one column per draw\n\nFor a .~ or looped y[i] ~ statement the missing data must have the output's shape, as fill(missing, n) does. A bare missing works for y ~. To free observations bound with condition, use predict(decondition(model), chn).","title":"Draw from the posterior predictive"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/predictive/#Compute-a-derived-quantity-per-draw","page":"Check a model with predictive draws","text":"A quantity assigned with := in the model, such as yhat := α .+ β .* x, is stored in the chain and recomputed by predict for new data. For anything else, end the model with return expr, for example return α + β for the fitted value at x = 1. returned gives its value at every draw, as a draws × chains matrix:\n\nfitted_at_1 = returned(model, chn)","title":"Compute a derived quantity per draw"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/predictive/#Get-pointwise-log-likelihoods-for-LOO","page":"Check a model with predictive draws","text":"pointwise_loglikelihoods gives one log-likelihood per observation at one draw; chain_draws turns the chain into a vector of draws. With flatten=true, stack them into the observations × draws matrix that PSIS-LOO tools such as ParetoSmooth.jl take:\n\nll = reduce(hcat, pointwise_loglikelihoods(model, d; flatten=true) for d in chain_draws(chn))\n\nFor the summed value at one point, use loglikelihood_at; for the log joint, logjoint.","title":"Get pointwise log-likelihoods for LOO"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/float32/#Run-a-model-in-Float32","page":"Run a model in Float32","text":"Three rules keep a model in Float32. Breaking one does not raise an error; the computation is silently promoted to Float64.\n\nWrite distribution constants as Float32 literals: Normal(0f0, 10f0), Exponential(1.0f0).\nCreate arrays from paramtype: zeros(paramtype(__mode__), k), not zeros(k).\nPass NUTS(0.8f0), not NUTS(0.8): the parameters take the sampler's number type, which comes from the target acceptance rate.\n\nData can stay Float64.\n\nusing PracticalBayes, Distributions, LinearAlgebra, StableRNGs, Statistics\n\n@model function reg32(X, y)\n    k = size(X, 2)\n    beta ~ MvNormal(zeros(paramtype(__mode__), k), 1f0 * I)\n    sigma ~ Exponential(1f0)\n    y ~ MvNormal(X * beta, sigma^2 * I)\nend\n\nX = randn(StableRNG(1), 100, 3)\ny = X * [1.0, -1.0, 0.5] .+ 0.2 .* randn(StableRNG(2), 100)\n\nchn = sample(StableRNG(3), reg32(X, y), NUTS(0.8f0), 500; progress=false)\nmean(chn[:beta])\n3-element Vector{Float32}:\n  0.9815317\n -1.0005999\n  0.5176598\n\nThe data were simulated with beta = [1, -1, 0.5]. Sampling runs in Float32, but a bounded scalar such as sigma is currently reported in the chain as Float64.\n\nTo evaluate the log density and its gradient in Float32 yourself, build the layout with T=Float32:\n\nlayout, θ0, store = build_layout(model; T=Float32)\nldf = LogDensityFunction(model, layout, store, AutoForwardDiff(); θ0=θ0)","title":"Run a model in Float32"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/trimming/#Compile-a-model-into-a-standalone-binary","page":"Compile a model into a standalone binary","text":"Julia 1.12's juliac --trim can compile a simple PracticalBayes model, its gradient and its sampler into an executable that runs without Julia installed. The gradient comes from Piste, PracticalBayes' trimmable AD backend.\n\nThis is experimental. It has been shown to work on small models like the one below; check your own model's binary against the same code run normally.","title":"Compile a model into a standalone binary"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/trimming/#Write-the-program","page":"Compile a model into a standalone binary","text":"Put the program in its own package, with PracticalBayes, Piste, Distributions, LogDensityProblems and Random as dependencies:\n\nmodule MyTrim\n\nusing PracticalBayes\nimport Piste\nusing Distributions: Normal, Exponential\nusing LogDensityProblems: LogDensityProblems\nusing Random: Xoshiro\n\n@static_model function simple(y)\n    mu ~ Normal(0, 1)\n    sigma ~ Exponential(1)\n    y ~ Normal(mu, sigma)\nend\n\n# A trimmed binary cannot inspect the model when it runs, so the log density\n# is built once, when the package is precompiled.\nconst M = simple(2.0)\nconst LAYOUT, THETA0, STORE0 = build_layout(M; rng=Xoshiro(1234))\nconst LDF = LogDensityFunction(M, LAYOUT, STORE0, AutoPBForwardDiff(chunk=2); θ0=THETA0)\n\nfunction logp_grad!(g::Vector{Float64}, x::Vector{Float64})\n    lp, grad = LogDensityProblems.logdensity_and_gradient(LDF, x)\n    copyto!(g, grad)\n    return lp\nend\n\nfunction (@main)(args::Vector{String})::Cint\n    res = static_hmc(logp_grad!, copy(THETA0), ones(length(THETA0)), 0.05, 16, 500, 200; seed=1)\n    println(Core.stdout, \"mean of mu: \", sum(res.draws[1, :]) / size(res.draws, 2))\n    return 0\nend\n\nend\n\nPoints to keep:\n\nDefine the model with @static_model, not @model.\nUse AutoPBForwardDiff for the gradient. Other AD backends, such as ForwardDiff, and the closed-form GradMode gradient do not trim.\nSample with static_hmc. sample and AdvancedHMC's samplers do not trim.\nSeed the RNG for the starting point, so every build starts from the same place.\nChoose the step size, step count and metric for static_hmc beforehand: run the program under Julia, with MyTrim.main(String[]), and check res.n_divergent and res.accept_rate.","title":"Write the program"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/trimming/#Build-and-run","page":"Compile a model into a standalone binary","text":"Install JuliaC.jl in an environment juliac-env, then:\n\njulia --project=juliac-env -e 'using JuliaC; JuliaC.main(ARGS)' -- \\\n    --output-exe mytrim --bundle build --trim=safe --experimental ./MyTrim\nJULIA_LOAD_CODEGEN_LIB=0 ./build/bin/mytrim\n\nA build with no verifier errors can still be wrong. Run the binary with JULIA_LOAD_CODEGEN_LIB=0 and check that its output matches MyTrim.main(String[]) run under Julia.","title":"Build and run"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/from_turing/#Port-a-model-from-Turing","page":"Port a model from Turing","text":"Most Turing models run unchanged, and the Turing tutorials can be followed with PracticalBayes in place of Turing.","title":"Port a model from Turing"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/from_turing/#Follow-a-Turing-tutorial","page":"Port a model from Turing","text":"Make three changes to the tutorial's code:\n\nReplace using Turing with using PracticalBayes, Distributions. PracticalBayes does not re-export Distributions.\nReplace @varname(x) with :x, since chains are indexed by symbol. An element such as chn[@varname(m[1])] becomes param_draws(chn, :m, 1).\nReplace VNChain with SymChain where a tutorial names the chain type.\n\nWith only these changes, these tutorials run: linear regression, logistic regression, Poisson regression, multinomial logistic regression, time series analysis, coin flipping, Bayesian neural networks, differential equations, Gaussian processes (introduction) and probabilistic PCA.\n\nGaussian mixture models and hidden Markov models also need the indexed form of change 2 for their plots; they sample with PG. For a hidden Markov model, an exact forward-filtering backward-sampling kernel is the better choice; see Sample with Gibbs blocks.\n\nInfinite mixture models (SMC, Turing.RandomMeasures) and variational inference (Turing.Variational) are not supported.","title":"Follow a Turing tutorial"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/from_turing/#Differences-to-know","page":"Port a model from Turing","text":"","title":"Differences to know"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/from_turing/#Prefer-.-to-a-loop-over-the-data","page":"Port a model from Turing","text":"y ~ dist, y .~ dist and y[i] ~ dist observe y when y is a model argument (or a := name), and declare parameters otherwise. An argument the body reassigns to a new container still counts as data. A loop of y[i] ~ works, but .~ over the whole vector is much faster, and it accepts one distribution per element where Turing needs product_distribution:\n\ny .~ Normal.(mu, sigma)\n\n.~ cannot declare parameters; use an array distribution with ~. For an IID vector prior, beta ~ MvNormal(...) is much faster under reverse-mode AD than a loop of beta[j] ~.","title":"Prefer .~ to a loop over the data"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/from_turing/#Predictive-sampling-needs-the-output-shape","page":"Port a model from Turing","text":"For a .~ or looped y[i] ~ observe, pass fill(missing, n) for the data, not a bare missing:\n\npredict(model(X, fill(missing, n)), chn)","title":"Predictive sampling needs the output shape"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/from_turing/#:-also-marks-data","page":"Port a model from Turing","text":"As in Turing, z := expr records z in the chain. It also makes z count as data, so a later z ~ dist observes it. See Data and parameters: :=.","title":":= also marks data"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/from_turing/#Only-whole-variables-can-be-conditioned","page":"Port a model from Turing","text":"model | (; y = data) works; model | (; x[3] = 5.0) does not.","title":"Only whole variables can be conditioned"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/from_turing/#Float32-needs-explicit-literals","page":"Port a model from Turing","text":"See Run a model in Float32.","title":"Float32 needs explicit literals"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/from_turing/#Using-both-packages-at-once","page":"Port a model from Turing","text":"PracticalBayes installs alongside current Turing, but many names, among them @model, NUTS, Gibbs, PG and filldist, are exported by both. With both loaded, import one and qualify its names:\n\nusing PracticalBayes\nimport Turing","title":"Using both packages at once"},{"category":"section","location":"howto/from_turing/#Vector-parameters-in-chains","page":"Port a model from Turing","text":"A vector parameter is one chain entry holding whole vectors, so chn[:beta][:, 2] is the second chain, not beta[2]. Use param_draws:\n\nparam_draws(chn, :beta, 2)","title":"Vector parameters in chains"},{"category":"section","location":"#PracticalBayes.jl","page":"Home","text":"PracticalBayes is a WIP probabilistic programming package for Julia. It is built largely from the building blocks of the Turing ecosystem (AbstractPPL, AdvancedHMC, Bijectors, Distributions, LogDensityProblems and FlexiChains), with its own model evaluation and some changes so that it works well with the PracticallyBayesian packages.\n\nModels are written with Turing-style @model and ~ syntax and sampled with HMC/NUTS, alone or as blocks of a Gibbs sampler.\n\nusing PracticalBayes, Distributions\n\n@model function linreg(x, y)\n    α ~ Normal(0, 10)\n    β ~ Normal(0, 10)\n    σ ~ Exponential(1)\n    y .~ Normal.(α .+ β .* x, σ)\nend\n\nchn = sample(linreg(x, y), NUTS(), 1000)","title":"PracticalBayes.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-is-different-here","page":"Home","text":"Gibbs kernels as plain functions. A Gibbs block can be your own AbstractGibbsKernel: one gibbs_step method that receives the model's data and the current value of every parameter, and returns new values for its block. It runs once per MCMC step, outside the gradient. It is similar to Turing's GibbsConditional and externalsampler, but designed to be easy to use with the latent-state samplers of epidemic models, such as those in EpidemicTrajectories.jl. See Sample with Gibbs blocks.\nFloat32. A model can run in Float32 end to end, with a few rules about literals. See Run a model in Float32.\nTrimmable models. A simple model can be compiled into a standalone binary with juliac --trim, with some restrictions. See Compile a model into a standalone binary.\nGPU data (untested). Data can be held on a CUDA device. This has not yet been run on GPU hardware and is a work in progress. See Keep data on a GPU.","title":"What is different here"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":"New to the package: Getting started.\nComing from Turing: Port a model from Turing. The Turing tutorials can be followed with PracticalBayes with minimal changes.\nHow the pieces fit together: the Explanation.","title":"Where to go next"}]
}
